-\title{SoK: When Do LLM Side-Channel Defenses Protect Agent Workflows?\\A Result-Record Repository, Comparability Audit and Boundary Analysis}
+\title{SoK: Evidence Boundaries of LLM and Agent Side-Channel Defenses\\A Result-Record Audit and Two Controlled Case Studies}
 \author{}
 
 \begin{document}
 \maketitle
 
 \begin{abstract}
-When does a side-channel defense evaluated on an isolated LLM component protect an autonomous agent workflow? Unlike single-turn inference, an agent workflow couples a \emph{multi-hop observation path} (where downstream renderers or backward passes can re-emit protected signals) with a \emph{stateful execution path} (where cover actions interact with live tools and GUIs). Rather than claiming new security axioms beyond end-to-end placement, state isolation or adaptive evaluation, we contribute a source-bound \emph{result-record repository} (\FinalN{} study families: \FinalCore{} core, \FinalAdjacent{} adjacent; 39 intervention result records across 78 utility/cost fields), a nine-item comparability audit and controlled boundary tests across both paths. Each record binds its threat polarity (Type~I confidentiality, Type~II attribution, Type~III bot-detection evasion, Type~IV diagnostics), final receiving projection, cover execution semantics, and matched adaptation ($D_2$) and cost objects. On a 24-scenario retrospective deployment-transfer benchmark, two independent expert coders applying this three-check record procedure reach 93.8\% mean accuracy (95.8\% and 91.7\%, catching 100\% and 91.7\% of documented deployment failures; inter-coder agreement 87.5\%, $\kappa=0.795$), versus 29.2\% (0\% failure recall) for a deterministic headline-metric reuse baseline. Independent dual-coding across 100 screening records ($\kappa=0.886$), 144 split-audit cells ($\kappa=0.876$) and 150 pairwise judgments ($\kappa=0.916$) establishes coding reliability. Real loopback TLS~1.2 replays across 48 saved responses (four natural task-granularity regimes, $d\in[0.25,10.4]$) and joint privacy--utility evaluations across 115 $\tau$-bench retail reference tasks (97 tasks with an eligible pending order, $\times 3$ seeds $\times 2$ insertion positions) establish three synthesis insights: (1) required terminal framing overhead scales with natural target length separability ($512$B bucketing eliminates medium/short exposition leakage at $0.87\times$--$0.91\times$ baseline wire bytes, whereas directive verbosity requires $1{,}024$B bucketing at $1.94\times$ or constant capping at $23.37\times$); (2) in stateful agent tools, \emph{payload idempotence does not imply state-machine idempotence}---mapping an order item to its own identical \texttt{item\_id} ($\Delta\text{price}=0$) still triggers a one-way \texttt{"pending (item modified)"} status transition that breaks 59.45\% of eligible pre-insertion tasks, whereas state-idempotent address writes and decoy-account isolation preserve 100\% user-account state; and (3) retraining an adaptive observer ($D_2$) rebounds attacker accuracy by $+20.8$ to $+21.1$ percentage points on bucketed/stochastic TLS streams and $+22.5$ to $+49.0$ points on retail tool-dispatch traces relative to a clean-trained ($D_0$) observer.
+Published side-channel defenses protect specific observations and execution environments. Reusing these results in LLM-based workflows requires identifying which guarantees survive changes in receivers, tool state and attacker adaptation. We systematize \FinalN{} study families (\FinalCore{} core and \FinalAdjacent{} adjacent), retaining 39 scoped result records and auditing 20 mitigation families. A nine-item reporting audit and three deployment checks bind each result to its target, observer, execution boundary, workload and measured cost. The audit distinguishes established incompatibility from missing or unverified evidence; only 3 of 17 applicable families bind task or component utility, and 2 of 11 bind a matched adaptation contrast. Two controlled case studies illustrate the resulting evaluation obligations. Replays of 48 saved responses over two TLS~1.2 links reproduce downstream re-emission after upstream length padding. With matched workload denominators, 512-byte buckets cost 2.30$\times$ and 1.43$\times$ for two sentence-prefix workloads; a cap fitted to training lengths reaches 50\% test accuracy at 2.18$\times$ for the verbosity target. Replaying 115 retail reference programs shows that same-item writes can change workflow state, and that preserving task objects differs from preserving an account or a shared database. Simple count or position rules recover cancellation labels despite cover calls. These are local mechanism tests, not a combined autonomous-agent evaluation. The contribution is a reusable evidence audit that identifies which comparisons are supported and which require new measurements.
 \end{abstract}
 
