 \section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}
-Encryption conceals message content, yet LLM serving engines and autonomous agents expose rich side-channel projections: packet-size streams, KV-cache hit/miss timing, hardware memory-access patterns, visited web domains and tool-invocation sequences \cite{weiss2024prompt,promptpeek2025,earlybird2025,agentprint2025,uitraces2026,jeong2026wra}. As the literature has grown rapidly, system designers face an acute \emph{composition fallacy}: defenses evaluated on an isolated LLM component or offline trace are routinely assumed to protect an end-to-end agent workflow.
+Encryption conceals message content, yet LLM serving engines and agent systems expose packet sizes, cache timing, memory-access patterns, visited domains and tool-invocation sequences \cite{weiss2024prompt,promptpeek2025,earlybird2025,agentprint2025,uitraces2026,jeong2026wra}. A published defense result applies to a particular observer and operating point. Reusing it across a frontend, a tool dispatcher or another workload can change both the observable signal and the cost being measured. We study this interpretation problem without assuming that the original authors claimed end-to-end protection.
 
-Why does a component defense result fail to transfer unchanged to an agent workflow? Synthesizing \FinalN{} study families (\FinalCore{} core, \FinalAdjacent{} adjacent), we show that embedding an LLM inside an agent workflow introduces three structural boundaries that isolated benchmarks do not test together:
+Our systematization asks which evidence permits such reuse. It separates three obligations:
 \begin{enumerate}
-\item \textbf{The Observation Projection Boundary (Trace Path):} An agent workflow chains serving backends, orchestration layers, content-aware rendering frontends and split-model return paths. Upstream traffic shaping preserves its information bound ($I(S;Y)\le I(S;Z)$, Proposition~1) \emph{only} if downstream stages are target-oblivious postprocessors. When a content-aware frontend decrypts, strips padding and re-chunks decoded tokens \cite{netecho2025}, or when a backward pass returns gradients with unprotected nonzero row support \cite{politis2026splitdecoy}, it creates a secondary emitter that restores target distinguishability.
-\item \textbf{The Action State-Machine Boundary (Execution Path):} In classical network privacy or read-only retrieval (such as the Web and Research Agents study, WRA \cite{jeong2026wra}), ``cover traffic'' consists of read-only dummy queries whose responses are discarded locally. In an autonomous agent workflow, admitting cover actions to live tool or GUI dispatch interacts with \emph{stateful external environments} (such as the Agent Humanization Benchmark, AHB \cite{zhu2026turing}, or transactional tool APIs). Crucially, we uncover \textbf{the Idempotence Illusion}: in stateful tool APIs, \emph{argument-level payload idempotence does not imply state-machine idempotence}. Calling a tool to replace an item with its own identical \texttt{item\_id} at zero price difference still triggers one-way workflow status transitions (\texttt{"pending (item modified)"}) and append-only ledger entries that silently break subsequent user actions.
-\item \textbf{The Evaluation Coupling Boundary ($D_2$--Utility--Cost Trilemma):} Across the 20 core families that evaluate mitigations (39 result records across 78 utility/cost fields), studies routinely measure one vertex of the security--utility--cost triangle against a static clean-trained ($D_0$) observer and extrapolate to whole-workflow protection---omitting either adaptive retraining on the defended distribution ($D_2$), objective task-state preservation, or workload-level cache-reuse loss \cite{earlybird2025,addagada2026kvgov,spliceleak2026}.
+\item \textbf{Bind the receiving projection.} Target-oblivious postprocessing is a sufficient condition for preserving an upstream information bound (Proposition~1). A frontend with access to decoded content or an unprotected backward computation requires a separate argument or measurement \cite{netecho2025,politis2026splitdecoy}.
+\item \textbf{Bind execution and protected state.} Read-only decoy requests and GUI actions operate on different interfaces and serve different objectives \cite{jeong2026wra,zhu2026turing}. For tools, unchanged argument values do not imply unchanged database state. The relevant contract must identify task objects, the user's account and shared state separately.
+\item \textbf{Bind adaptation, utility and cost.} An attack on defended data must specify how the attacker was fitted; an overhead must identify the same workload and deployed mechanism as the claimed protection. Missing bindings limit reuse without establishing that the original study omitted or failed the experiment.
 \end{enumerate}
 
-\paragraph{Positioning: a result-record repository and comparability audit}
-We do not claim to invent the end-to-end principle, state isolation or adaptive evaluation; original study authors frequently note local instances of these phenomena. Rather, our contribution is systematizing the fragmented LLM and agent side-channel literature into a \textbf{source-bound result-record repository, a nine-item comparability audit, and a three-check operational procedure} that lets practitioners and reviewers determine exactly when a published measurement transfers to a target workflow. Without structured record binding, relying on paper-level headline metrics fails systematically: on a 24-scenario retrospective deployment-transfer benchmark drawn from our corpus, an objective baseline rule that accepts any defense reporting favorable local attack suppression or $<10\%$ component overhead achieves only 29.2\% accuracy (7/24) and catches \textbf{0\% (0/12)} of documented deployment failures. By contrast, two independent expert coders applying our three-check record procedure reach \textbf{95.8\% (23/24)} and \textbf{91.7\% (22/24)} accuracy (mean \textbf{93.8\%}, $+64.6$ percentage points over baseline; 87.5\% inter-coder agreement, $\kappa=0.795$).
-
-\paragraph{Summary of SoK contributions}
+\paragraph{Contribution and scope}
+The end-to-end principle, state isolation and adaptive evaluation are established ideas. Our contribution is their application to a source-bound repository of heterogeneous LLM and agent measurements. We provide:
 \begin{itemize}
-\item \textbf{Unified 82-family repository and threat-polarity taxonomy (Sections~\ref{sec:methodology}--\ref{sec:corpus-map}):} We index \FinalN{} study families (\FinalCore{} core, \FinalAdjacent{} adjacent) and 39 intervention result records (78 utility/cost fields across all \FinalCore{} core families), explicitly separating (Type~I) confidentiality protection, (Type~II) provenance/identity attribution, (Type~III) bot-detection evasion, and (Type~IV) authorized operator diagnostics. Independent human/expert dual-coding across 100 screening records ($\kappa=0.886$), 144 split-audit cells ($\kappa=0.876$) and 150 pairwise comparability judgments ($\kappa=0.916$) establishes high interpretive reliability.
-\item \textbf{Nine-item split reporting audit and retrospective transfer benchmark (Sections~\ref{sec:scope} and \ref{sec:defenses}):} We split adaptation into protected-condition fitting ($D_1$, Check~4a) versus matched clean-versus-adapted testing ($D_2$, Check~4b), and decompose joint evaluation into defended inference effect (Check~6a), task/component utility (Check~6b) and mechanism/protocol cost (Check~6c) across all 20 evaluated-mitigation families, showing that while 85\% bind inference effects and 55\% bind component cost, only 15\% bind utility, 10\% bind $D_2$ adaptation, and only one family (\texttt{CachePrune} \cite{wu2026cacheprune}) binds all three on the same operating point.
-\item \textbf{Real TLS~1.2 socket replays and joint privacy--utility retail cover experiments across all three checks (Section~\ref{sec:controlled-placement}):} (i) Using real loopback TLS~1.2 sockets across 48 unmodified \texttt{gpt-6-luna} outputs spanning four natural task-granularity regimes ($d\in[0.25,10.4]$), we map how bucketed terminal framing trades wire overhead ($0.87\times$--$1.94\times$) against natural target length separability and quantify the $D_0\to D_2$ adaptation rebound ($+20.8$ to $+21.1$pp); and (ii) across 115 $\tau$-bench retail reference tasks (97 tasks with an eligible pending order, $\times 3$ seeds $\times 2$ insertion positions), we jointly evaluate task utility and $D_0$/$D_2$ observer inference across nine baseline and realistic cover policies (including state-idempotent address writes, payload-idempotent item self-writes, an oracle-free instruction-aware cover selector and decoy-account isolation).
+\item \textbf{A result-record repository:} \FinalN{} study families (\FinalCore{} core and \FinalAdjacent{} adjacent), with 39 scoped records and 78 utility/cost field positions from 26 families, including mitigation and contextual comparison records. The reporting audit covers 20 mitigation families. Records separate source versions, receiver views, execution modes and cost objects that a paper-level score can conflate.
+\item \textbf{A comparability audit:} nine reporting items, numbered R1--R9, and three operational checks. Among applicable families, utility is bound in 3/17 and a matched adaptation contrast in 2/11. The latter includes a capture-based result and does not establish live-protocol adaptation. A 24-scenario author-constructed exercise illustrates rule application; it is not an external validation benchmark.
+\item \textbf{Two controlled case studies:} a TLS~1.2 replay of saved responses and a separate replay of 115 retail reference programs. They test receiving boundaries, state scopes and attacker-view choices. They neither execute an autonomous LLM agent nor measure the joint protection of the two paths.
 \end{itemize}
 
