-\section{Implications, Ethical Boundaries and Limits}\label{sec:agenda}
-\subsection{A nine-item deployment reporting checklist}
-To make future defense evaluations reusable across agent workflows without forcing every component paper to run an end-to-end benchmark, studies should report the nine split fields audited in Table~\ref{tab:six-check-audit}: (1) hidden target and threat polarity (Types~I--IV); (2) final receiver fields and session/document linkage permissions; (3) where protection terminates and whether cover reaches live tool/GUI dispatch; (4a) protected-condition fitting/calibration ($D_1$) and (4b) matched clean-versus-adapted testing ($D_2$) on identical defended units; (5) tested workloads and generator holdouts; and (6a) defended inference outcome, (6b) task or component utility (distinguishing task-touched state from whole-account and global state), and (6c) the specific mechanism or protocol cost object measured.
+\section{Implications, Ethics and Limits}\label{sec:agenda}
+\subsection{Reporting obligations and open problems}
+The nine audit items identify evidence needed for particular transfer claims: R1 target and protected interest; R2 receiver fields and linkage; R3 intervention placement and execution; R4 defended-condition fitting; R5 matched adaptation contrast; R6 workload and generator shifts; and R7--R9 inference, utility and cost. They do not require every component study to implement an autonomous agent. Inapplicable and unverified items must remain distinct from unreported evidence.
 
-\subsection{Open research problems implied by the systematization}
-Five concrete research problems emerge from our repository and boundary experiments:
-\begin{enumerate}
-\item \textbf{End-to-end target-oblivious streaming protocols:} How can content-aware SSE/WebSocket renderers and split-model backward passes enforce the Markov condition of Proposition~1 ($I(S;Y\mid Z)=0$) using adaptive bucketed framing calibrated to task length entropy ($d\in[0.25,10.4]$)?
-\item \textbf{Formal state-idempotence contracts for tool schemas:} Can MCP and tool-calling specifications distinguish pure reads, true state-idempotent overwrites (\texttt{modify\_\allowbreak pending\_\allowbreak order\_\allowbreak address}), and status-mutating or ledger-appending writes (\texttt{modify\_\allowbreak pending\_\allowbreak order\_\allowbreak items}) so cover dispatchers can verify state neutrality before invocation?
-\item \textbf{Constant-count decoy-account transactions against $D_2$ observers:} Because a $D_2$ observer rebounds from 22.45\% to 71.43\% accuracy on single-cover retail sessions by counting total writes per episode (Table~\ref{tab:tau-cover-bridge}), how can sandboxed decoy accounts equalize total write counts across sessions without exhausting shared database locks?
-\item \textbf{Workload-level KV-cache reuse pricing:} What is the multi-turn cache-miss latency penalty of cryptographic namespace isolation (KVGov \cite{addagada2026kvgov}) relative to selective sensitive-token pruning (CachePrune \cite{wu2026cacheprune}) under realistic agent trajectories?
-\item \textbf{Cross-generator invariance:} Which behavioural or traffic features survive simultaneous shifts in task family, browser configuration and multi-agent topology?
-\end{enumerate}
+Three priorities follow. First, terminal streaming protocols should account for new target-bearing inputs at each frontend, using realistic length tails and timing observations. Second, tool contracts should state the protected state scope and successful/rejected-call effects; same-value payloads and repeated-call idempotence are different from a no-change guarantee. Third, cover evaluation should include policy-aware count and sequence attacks before describing a privacy--utility frontier. Constant write counts alone need not hide order, names, identifiers or response lengths. Workload-level cache-reuse costs and cross-generator fingerprint portability remain separate open problems \cite{addagada2026kvgov,wu2026cacheprune}.
 
-\subsection{Dual-use, bot-detection evasion and ethical boundaries}\label{sec:ethics}
-Separating threat polarities (Types~I--IV) exposes a critical dual-use and ethical boundary that is obscured when all side-channel interventions are labeled ``privacy defenses.'' Type~I defenses (such as TLS/OHTTP record padding in NetEcho and Dangol, selective KV sharing in CachePrune, and local read-only query decoys in WRA) protect legitimate users from passive network profiling or co-tenant prompt extraction. By contrast, Type~III action humanization (AHB \cite{zhu2026turing}) is explicitly designed to make automated GUI agents indistinguishable from human users so they bypass platform bot detection and anti-automation controls.
+\subsection{Ethics and artifact scope}\label{sec:ethics}
+The revision executes only local loopback sockets and cloned public benchmark databases. It invokes no production tool, sends no cover action to a real account and makes no new model API call. The synthetic and retail programs are prescribed reference executions; they are not observations of real user consent. The other-account cancellation condition is a simulator control, not a recommendation to cancel orders belonging to other users. A deployable decoy would require explicitly controlled resources and a separate isolation argument.
 
-Deploying behavioural mimicry or synthetic GUI touch injection to evade bot detection carries two significant risks: (1) \textbf{erosion of platform accountability and abuse controls}, as websites and mobile platforms rely on behavioural telemetry (UI Traces, FP-Agent, WebAgents \cite{uitraces2026,wang2026fpagent,fayolle2026webagents}) to defend against credential stuffing, automated scraping and fraud; and (2) \textbf{third-party state and safety hazards}, as injecting live synthetic actions into production interfaces risks triggering unauthorized transactions or corrupting user state (demonstrated by AHB's 0.75 to 0.15 Trip Planning collapse and our retail state-mutation findings). System designers seeking legitimate Type~I user privacy should therefore prefer protocol-level transport shaping (TLS/OHTTP bucketing), shadow-clone execution, or isolated decoy accounts rather than deceptive GUI manipulation against third-party services.
+The corpus includes both confidentiality protection and bot-detection evasion. Authorization and protected interests must be stated: AHB's humanization objective differs from user privacy against a passive observer \cite{zhu2026turing,jeong2026wra}. Source analysis does not authorize evasion of third-party controls. The local artifact retains saved model outputs and their source provenance; model availability, terms at the historical generation date and fresh-generation reproducibility have not been independently revalidated in this revision. An accessible immutable anonymous repository remains a submission task; local files and hashes do not constitute a public release.
 
