-\begin{enumerate}
-\item \textbf{The Idempotence Illusion (\texttt{live\_\allowbreak idempotent\_\allowbreak address} vs.\ \texttt{live\_\allowbreak idempotent\_\allowbreak item\_\allowbreak swap}):} Writing an order's current shipping address back via \texttt{modify\_\allowbreak pending\_\allowbreak order\_\allowbreak address} overwrites the address dict in place without altering \texttt{status} or \texttt{payment\_history}, preserving \textbf{100.0\% native reward} (\texttt{before} and \texttt{after}). By contrast, calling \texttt{modify\_\allowbreak pending\_\allowbreak order\_\allowbreak items} to map an order's first item to its \emph{own identical} \texttt{item\_id} (\texttt{new\_item\_ids=[item\_id]}, $\Delta\text{price}=\$0.00$) is payload-idempotent on item values, yet in $\tau$-bench's state machine any successful item modification transitions \texttt{order["status"]} from \texttt{"pending"} to \texttt{"pending (item modified)"} (blocking any subsequent modification or cancellation!) and appends a $\$0.00$ refund record. It mutates state in \textbf{59.45\% (173/291)} of \texttt{before} eligible units, collapsing eligible native reward to \textbf{40.55\%} (\texttt{before}, CI $[31.62\%,50.17\%]$) and \textbf{67.70\%} (\texttt{after}, CI $[59.10\%,76.29\%]$), while providing \textbf{0\% privacy protection} against a cancellation-verb observer (100.0\% $D_0$ and $D_2$ accuracy).
-\item \textbf{The Account-Scope Hazard under Oracle-Free Instruction Parsing (\texttt{live\_\allowbreak instruction\_\allowbreak aware}):} To test realistic context-aware cover without oracle access to future reference actions (\texttt{task.actions}), \texttt{live\_\allowbreak instruction\_\allowbreak aware} parses \emph{only} the user's natural-language prompt (\texttt{task.instruction}) and initial order table, selecting a pending order whose ID and item names are not mentioned in \texttt{task.instruction} (available in 70.10\% [68/97] of eligible tasks) and falling back to state-idempotent address writes otherwise. Because it avoids orders mentioned by the user, \textbf{task-target state preservation (\texttt{gold\_touched\_state\_equal}) rises from 62.54\% (blind cancel) to 78.35\% (\texttt{before}, CI $[70.10\%,86.60\%]$) and 89.69\% (\texttt{after}, CI $[83.51\%,94.85\%]$)}! Yet because cancelling even an \emph{unmentioned} pending order on the same user's account deletes a real bystander order owned by that user, whole-account \texttt{native\_reward} remains \textbf{35.05\% (\texttt{before}) and 46.39\% (\texttt{after})}.
-\item \textbf{Decoy-Account Isolation and the $D_0\to D_2$ Observer Rebound (\texttt{live\_\allowbreak decoy\_\allowbreak account}):} Routing live \texttt{cancel\_\allowbreak pending\_\allowbreak order} cover calls to a designated decoy user account preserves \textbf{100.0\% of the active user's account state (\texttt{user\_orders\_state\_equal = 100.0\%})} and \textbf{100.0\% of task-touched state}. On the privacy axis, injecting live cancellations reduces a clean-trained $D_0$ observer's accuracy from \textbf{100.0\% to 51.02\%} (\texttt{live\_\allowbreak instruction\_\allowbreak aware}) and \textbf{22.45\%} (\texttt{live\_\allowbreak decoy\_\allowbreak account} / \texttt{live\_\allowbreak mutation}). However, when the observer retrains on the defended tool-dispatch distribution ($D_2$), it exploits session write counts and payload byte lengths to rebound to \textbf{73.47\% (\texttt{before}) / 71.43\% (\texttt{after})} under \texttt{live\_\allowbreak instruction\_\allowbreak aware} ($+22.45$pp gain) and \textbf{71.43\% (\texttt{before}) / 68.03\% (\texttt{after})} under \texttt{live\_\allowbreak decoy\_\allowbreak account} and \texttt{live\_\allowbreak mutation} ($+48.98$pp and $+45.58$pp adaptation rebound).
-\end{enumerate}
+The other-account condition selects a pending order belonging to another simulated user. It is not a provisioned dedicated decoy account. It preserves the active user's complete account and reference-changed objects in these tasks, while changing the shared database in every eligible unit (0\% database equality). Thus account isolation of an effect cannot be described as whole-database preservation or an authorized production defense.
 
-\soktakeaway{SoK Takeaway 4 (The Idempotence Illusion \& Joint Privacy--Utility Frontier)}{In stateful agent workflows, payload-idempotent tool calls (\texttt{new\_item\_ids=[item\_id]}) can corrupt 59.45\% of tasks via one-way status flags while yielding 0\% verb-level privacy; oracle-free instruction parsing protects 78.35\%--89.69\% of task-touched state but still damages bystander orders on the user's account; and even when decoy-account isolation preserves 100\% of user state, a $D_2$ adaptive observer recovers $+48.98$pp over a naive $D_0$ observer unless total write counts per session are equalized.}
+\paragraph{Observer permissions and simple attacks}
+The secret label is whether the reference program \emph{attempts} a cancellation, including a call that may fail after cover execution. The observer sees the ordered dispatched tool names, write counts and response lengths; failed calls remain visible. It sees no arguments, user/order identifiers or database state. We do not infer that a passive TLS observer has these permissions. A backend with plaintext state would instead need a different confidentiality target.
+
+The fixed task split contains 48 training tasks (7 positive, 41 negative) and 49 test tasks (11 positive, 38 negative); seeds stay within tasks. Always predicting no cancellation gives 77.55\% test accuracy and 50\% balanced accuracy. The legacy $D_2$ linear classifier uses cancellation presence, write count and write-response bytes. Its 68.03--73.47\% accuracy under cancellation cover is below the majority baseline, and its balanced accuracy ranges from 46.05\% to 52.51\%. An increase over $D_0$ is therefore insufficient evidence for its claimed privacy interpretation.
+
+For blind and other-account cancellation, exactly one extra cancellation is dispatched in every eligible session. The permitted rule $\widehat y=\mathbf{1}[N_{\rm cancel}\ge2]$ recovers the reference label at 100\% accuracy and balanced accuracy on all 49 test tasks and three seeds, before and after insertion. For any policy with one live cover call at a known first/last position, removing that call and testing for a remaining cancellation also gives 100\%. It needs no identifiers, no response content and no training labels. These are measured finite-sample results and a structural property of the fixed insertion design, not proof of general attack performance. Identifier filtering is unnecessary in this view and is not claimed as an evaluated attack.
+
+\paragraph{Terminal equality does not establish path properties}
+Blind pre-insertion cancellation leaves the final database equal to reference in 33/291 units across 12 tasks, even though it preempts the later reference cancellation. The exact per-task counts are in Supplement S9. After insertion, 120/291 cover calls are rejected because reference execution already changed the order's status, and those rejections explain the 41.24\% equality rate. Rejection is not successful benign cover. In the 82 tasks with no reference execution error (246 units), before/after equality is 24/246 (9.76\%) and 94/246 (38.21\%). These results illustrate the limits of endpoint scoring; consent and authorization were not measured.
+
+\soktakeaway{SoK Takeaway 4 (State preservation and inference are separate)}{Same-value tool inputs can change workflow state. Equality on reference-changed objects can miss damage elsewhere in an account; account equality can coexist with global database changes. Even a state-preserving route need not hide a cancellation label from the specified dispatch observer. The tested policies do not establish a privacy--utility frontier.}
